ctional-cto/", "/technical-due-diligence/"], "citations": [{"title": "Introducing Gateway API Inference Extension", "url": "https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/06/05/introducing-gateway-api-inference-extension/", "publisher": "Kubernetes blog", "date": "5 Jun 2025"}, {"title": "InferencePool", "url": "https://gateway-api-inference-extension.sigs.k8s.io/api-types/inferencepool/", "publisher": "Gateway API Inference Extension documentation"}, {"title": "Gateway API Inference Extension", "url": "https://gateway-api-inference-extension.sigs.k8s.io/", "publisher": "Kubernetes SIG Network"}, {"title": "Inference routing", "url": "https://agentgateway.dev/docs/kubernetes/main/inference/", "publisher": "agentgateway documentation"}, {"title": "Gateway API", "url": "https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/gateway/", "publisher": "Kubernetes documentation"}]}-->
<p>When teams first put a self-hosted model behind Kubernetes, they usually reach for the familiar pattern: a Deployment of model servers, a Service in front, and a load balancer spreading requests evenly. It works in a demo. Under real load, some replicas sit with long queues and nearly full GPU memory while others are idle, and tail latency climbs even though the cluster as a whole has spare capacity.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why LLM traffic breaks ordinary load balancing</h2>
<p>The Kubernetes project's introduction to the Inference Extension puts it plainly: LLM inference sessions are often long-running, resource-intensive and partially stateful, and a single GPU-backed server may hold several sessions and in-memory token caches. Load balancers that route by HTTP path or round-robin don't account for model identity, request criticality or the current state of each server<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Two requests to the same model can differ in cost by orders of magnitude, depending on prompt and output length. And as covered in <a href="/insights/llm-inference-serving-vllm/">LLM inference serving</a>, sending a request to a replica that already holds its prompt prefix in cache is far cheaper than sending it anywhere else.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How the extension works</h2>
<p>The Gateway API Inference Extension builds on <a href="/insights/ingress-nginx-to-gateway-api/">Gateway API</a>, keeping the familiar Gateway and HTTPRoute model and adding inference-specific steps<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Its central resource is the <strong>InferencePool</strong>: a pool of model-server pods on shared compute, similar to a Service but aware of model serving<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The project documentation lists InferencePool as generally available since v1.0.0<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/kubernetes-inference-gateway/resource-model.webp" alt="Diagram of the Gateway API Inference Extension resource model: cluster operators own the Gateway, application developers own the HTTPRoute, inference platform owners own the InferencePool, and inference workload owners own InferenceModel resources beneath it" width="800" height="572" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The resource model as introduced in 2025: platform operators manage pools of model servers; model owners manage what is served. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/06/05/introducing-gateway-api-inference-extension/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Introducing Gateway API Inference Extension</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The original design separated responsibilities by persona: platform operators manage where and how models are served through pools, while model owners manage what is served, including traffic splitting between versions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The model-facing APIs have continued to evolve, so check the current documentation for your version.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The request flow</h2>
<p>A request reaches the gateway, which matches an HTTPRoute to an InferencePool. Instead of forwarding to any pod, the gateway consults an endpoint selection extension, often called the endpoint picker, which examines live pod metrics such as queue length, memory use and loaded adapters, and chooses the pod that can serve the request most efficiently<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Gateway implementations describe the picker tracking signals such as KV-cache utilisation and queue length<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/kubernetes-inference-gateway/request-flow.webp" alt="Diagram of the inference request flow: a client completion request reaches the Kubernetes Gateway, which uses body-based routing to select an InferencePool by model name and an endpoint selection step to pick the least-loaded model replica, then routes to that replica in the pool" width="802" height="751" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Request flow with the endpoint selection extension choosing a model-server pod based on live metrics. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/06/05/introducing-gateway-api-inference-extension/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Introducing Gateway API Inference Extension</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>To the client it's still one ordinary request. The intelligence lives in the gateway layer, where the platform team controls it.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What the benchmark showed</h2>
<p>The project compared the extension with a standard Kubernetes Service for a vLLM deployment on H100 GPUs, ramping traffic from 100 to 1,000 queries per second. Throughput was roughly on par, but the extension showed significantly lower p90 per-output-token latency at higher load, and lower end-to-end tail latency as traffic rose beyond about 400 to 500 queries per second<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The authors attribute this to avoiding hotspots that ordinary load balancing creates for long-running inference requests<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Tail latency is what users feel, and GPU hotspots are what finance pays for. Model-aware routing improves both without buying another GPU.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I'd adopt it</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Start from Gateway API.</strong> If your clusters still use an Ingress controller, the move to Gateway API is the prerequisite and has its own value.</li>
<li><strong>Pick a gateway implementation</strong> that supports the Inference Extension, and confirm which version of the APIs it implements.</li>
<li><strong>Expose metrics from your model servers</strong> that the endpoint picker needs, and check them under load before trusting the routing.</li>
<li><strong>Load test against your own traffic</strong>, comparing tail latency and cost per request with your current setup, as in <a href="/insights/llm-inference-serving-vllm/">LLM inference serving</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Use pools to separate concerns</strong>: interactive and batch traffic, regulated and general workloads, or different hardware classes.</li>
<li><strong>Combine with scheduling and cost controls</strong>: GPU allocation through <a href="/insights/kubernetes-dra-gpu-scheduling/">DRA</a>, training jobs through <a href="/insights/kubernetes-gang-scheduling-ai/">gang scheduling</a>, and spend visibility through <a href="/insights/focus-finops-ai-cost-allocation/">FOCUS-based cost allocation</a>.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where it fits in a private AI platform</h2>
<p>An inference gateway is the routing layer in the platform described in <a href="/insights/private-llm-on-prem/">private LLMs on-prem</a> and <a href="/insights/uae-ai-infrastructure-where-to-run-models/">where to run models in the UAE</a>. Above it sits the policy gateway that handles authentication, model approval and logging; below it, the serving engines and GPUs. Keeping those layers distinct makes each easier to change.</p>
</section>

